<section id="about"><div class="wrap about reveal">
  <div>
    <div class="kicker">The Mint</div>
    <h2>We make the souvenir<br>for time you'll never get back.</h2>
    <p>WastedToken Mint started in a two-car garage in Providence with a 20-ton arbor press, a stack of
      C260 brass blanks, and a bet that people still want to <strong>hold</strong> the things they're proud of.
      Nine years later we still cut every die in-house.</p>
    <p>Each coin begins as a hand-modelled relief sunk into hardened tool steel, then struck
      <strong>three times</strong> under 180 tonnes so the high points come up sharp and the fields stay
      mirror-flat. Then it's tumbled, antiqued by hand, and the relief is cut back on a lathe until the
      emblem catches raking light the way it should.</p>
    <p>We don't do open editions and we never restrike. When a run is gone it stays gone — the dies are
      defaced on camera and the plates archived in the public ledger. <strong>Scarcity is the point.</strong></p>
  </div>
